::CW_U03_L03_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.FIC.04] Source element: A character receives a warning before making a risky choice. Adaptation: A student ignores a teammate's warning about faulty robot code before the final round. What is preserved?{
=A. The warning before a risky choice.#Correct. The adaptation keeps the warning-and-choice pattern.
~B. The exact original setting.#Incorrect. The setting has been transformed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The character names from the source.#Incorrect. No original names are being preserved. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The complete original plot.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the plot situation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L03_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.FIC.04] Source element: A guide gives a traveler a protective object. Adaptation: A grandmother gives a narrator a recipe card before the narrator moves to a new city. What is transformed?{
~A. The source is copied exactly.#Incorrect. The object and situation are changed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The adaptation removes all source connection.#Incorrect. A guide gives an object before a difficult journey.
~C. The character has no journey.#Incorrect. Moving to a new city is a kind of journey.
=D. The protective object becomes emotional support through a recipe card.#Correct. The source object changes into a realistic family object with emotional meaning.
}

::CW_U03_L03_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.FIC.04] Adaptation plan: Keep the source pattern of a character tested by temptation, but set it in a school election where the character finds the answer key to a debate question. Which rationale is strongest?{
~A. I changed the names, so it is automatically new.#Incorrect. Names alone are not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I kept temptation and transformed the setting so the conflict feels realistic for a student character.#Correct. This names what is preserved, what changes, and why.
~C. I copied the whole source because old stories are always better.#Incorrect. Adaptation requires transformation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I removed the conflict so nobody has to choose.#Incorrect. The conflict is central to the adaptation.
}

::CW_U03_L03_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.FIC.04] A student adapts a sacred or culturally important source by mocking the belief system and using stereotypes. What is the best feedback?{
~A. This is strong because all adaptation should mock the source.#Incorrect. Adaptation should be purposeful and respectful, especially with sacred or cultural material.
~B. This is strong because stereotypes make writing clearer.#Incorrect. Stereotypes flatten people and can misrepresent the source.
=C. The student should revise to treat the source carefully, avoid stereotypes, and focus on a clear source element.#Correct. Respectful adaptation preserves craft learning while avoiding harmful distortion.
~D. The student should keep the mockery but change punctuation.#Incorrect. The issue is respectful treatment and representation, not punctuation.
}

::CW_U03_L03_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.FIC.04] Source element: A hero returns home changed after a difficult journey. Which adaptation best transforms the source element?{
=A. A student returns from a summer program with new confidence and sees the neighborhood differently.#Correct. This preserves return-with-change but transforms setting and situation.
~B. A character takes the same journey with the same events and same ending.#Incorrect. That is too close to copying. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A character has no journey, no return, and no change.#Incorrect. The source element disappears. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A story lists heroic vocabulary words only.#Incorrect. Vocabulary alone is not an adaptation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
